\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Exact mass normalization in two dimensions}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Let #{D_{m_0}} be dilation of the underlying square and lattice,
#{y=m_0x}, and let #{T_{m_0}} pull a field to the dilated lattice by
#{(T_{m_0}\phi)(y)=\phi(y/m_0)}. Put
##{\epsilon'=m_0\epsilon,\qquad\ell'=m_0\ell,\qquad
\lambda'=\lambda /m_0^2.}
This invertible linear map transports the full action and normalized
\vocabk{half-Dirichlet law}{fdip-00HX} to their mass-one versions
with parameters #{\epsilon',\ell',\lambda'}, without a field-amplitude
factor. It preserves the whole-space Wick variance, and
##{\Phi_\epsilon(f)=\Phi'_{\epsilon'}(f')\circ T_{m_0},
 \qquad f'(y)=m_0^{-2}f(y/m_0).}
It preserves the ultraviolet-then-volume order of limits.}

\proof{
\p{The incidence differences are unchanged by pullback. The mass
coefficient transforms as #{m_0^2\epsilon^2=(\epsilon')^2}, and
the quartic coefficient as #{\lambda\epsilon^2
=\lambda'(\epsilon')^2}. The Fourier formula of
\ref{fdip-00HY} depends on mass and mesh only through
#{m_0\epsilon}; hence #{c_\epsilon(m_0)=c_{\epsilon'}(1)}.
The trace polynomial defining the Wick term is therefore unchanged
apart from the displayed coefficient conversion.}

\p{The vertex measures transform by
#{(\epsilon')^2=m_0^2\epsilon^2}, giving the displayed test
pullback. Dilation carries the zero exterior values and the fixed
square to their dilated counterparts. The linear Jacobian is a
constant and cancels in the normalized law. At each fixed side length
the mesh still tends to zero first.}
}

\p{This is the direct dilation of the energy form and
\citet{(IV.6)-(IV.7)}{guerra1975euclideanII}. In mass-one units
the later periodic comparison uses #{\ell\ge2}; in the original
units these are squares of side at least #{2/m_0}.}
